## Writing Once, Using Everywhere

### The Setup

```xiom
interface Displayable {
  fn display(&self) -> Str;
}

type Book { title: Str; pages: Int; }
fn Book.display(&self) -> Str { self.title.clone() }

type Movie { name: Str; runtime: Int; }
fn Movie.display(&self) -> Str { self.name.clone() }
```

### The Generic Function

```xiom
fn print_all[T: Displayable](items: &Vec[T]) {
  let mut i = 0;
  while i < items.len() {
    io.println(items.get(i).unwrap().display());
    i += 1;
  }
}
```

This ONE function works with `Vec[Book]`, `Vec[Movie]`, and any future `Vec[Something]` where Something implements Displayable.

### Call It

```xiom
let books: Vec[Book] = Vec[Book].new();
books.push(Book{ title: "Dune", pages: 412 });
print_all(&books);
```

The compiler generates specialized code for each type at compile time (monomorphisation). No runtime cost.

